{"name":"Brilliant Oak","hex":"#8c6143","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brilliant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shining brightly.","Both bright and saturated."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A finely cut gemstone, especially a diamond, cut in a particular form with numerous facets so as to maximize light return through the top (called \"table\") of the stone.","One of many hummingbird species of the genus Heliodoxa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brilliant"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/120px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_O